1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,240
 In this video, we're going to talk about FDM 3D printing,

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,120
 one of my favorite manufacturing processes

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:06,400
 for working with plastic.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,760
 I am a massive fan of FDM 3D printers

5
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,000
 for a bunch of different reasons

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 that'll become apparent over the course of this video,

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,040
 but probably the most important reason,

8
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,080
 the reason why I'm so enamored with them

9
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:19,400
 is that FDM 3D printers have largely democratized

10
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,080
 manufacturing with plastic.

11
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:23,120
 These days, using an FDM 3D printer,

12
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,360
 you can make incredibly complicated shapes

13
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:29,200
 with basically no effort and very, very little cost.

14
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:30,720
 It used to be the case 10 years ago

15
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,000
 when I started undergrad that very few people

16
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,800
 had access to or had even used an FDM 3D printer before.

17
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,200
 Whereas these days, I think it's relatively uncommon

18
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,520
 if you get to university and do an engineering degree

19
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:45,720
 and you haven't had some experience with a 3D printer.

20
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,200
 You probably already know what it is and how it works.

21
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,720
 And most of you probably have had some experience

22
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:53,840
 using one at a school or a make space or a library

23
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:56,360
 or in your own house if you happen to own one.

24
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:57,960
 Nevertheless, given their ubiquity

25
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,040
 and the extreme affordability of this

26
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,520
 as a manufacturing technique for low-to-mid volume parts

27
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:05,680
 and certainly as a prototyping tool,

28
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:06,920
 we're going to spend a little bit of time

29
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,000
 in this video talking about how they work

30
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,080
 and some of the considerations that you need to make

31
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:13,120
 in your selection of a material for 3D printing

32
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:14,920
 and some of the considerations you need to make

33
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:17,240
 in designing components to be 3D printed.

34
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,720
 So we'll kick off by talking about some of the mechanics

35
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:21,280
 of how this all works.

36
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:24,240
 FDM is short for fused deposition modeling

37
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,120
 and the way it works is kind of given away in the name.

38
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:30,120
 Through a CNC process, a little bit like an old inkjet printer

39
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:33,360
 and FDM 3D printer works by tracing out a path

40
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:37,240
 in 3D space and depositing a layer of molten thermoplastic

41
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:38,440
 over this path.

42
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:42,160
 In practice, FDM 3D printers typically travel on 2D parts,

43
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:44,440
 tracing out layer by layer the cross section

44
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:46,200
 of whatever complicated shape it is

45
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,960
 that you'd like to 3D print.

46
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,480
 After completing a particular layer,

47
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:52,280
 the 3D printer will move the extruder away

48
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,120
 from that previously deposited layer

49
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,360
 and lay down the next one,

50
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,320
 whatever the next cross section along your part happens to be.

51
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,800
 Because the motion that an FDM 3D printer performs

52
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,000
 are reasonably straightforward,

53
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,200
 working layer by layer and stepping up

54
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:06,440
 and then working layer by layer and stepping up,

55
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:08,200
 they usually relatively straightforward

56
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,760
 and as a result have become very, very affordable.

57
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,320
 This affordability of the machines,

58
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,800
 coupled with the affordability of the 3D printer filament,

59
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,480
 which is usually just a cheap thermoplastic,

60
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:19,880
 coupled with the gain with the lack of complexity involved

61
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:22,200
 in writing the tool parts for these 3D printers

62
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:24,600
 because the movements are so simple,

63
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:26,120
 means that 3D printers have become

64
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:27,800
 pretty much an every man tool.

65
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,280
 And as a result, I've found favor amongst both those

66
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:32,200
 looking to rapid prototype their components

67
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:34,440
 and people looking to make finished parts

68
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:36,200
 at low to medium volume.

69
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,960
 To give you one example in NUT which is a pretty small

70
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,960
 engineering organization when you think about it,

71
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:42,720
 we have a small 3D print farm

72
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:45,520
 and we rapid prototype pretty much every component

73
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,600
 before it goes out for final manufacture.

74
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,400
 This means for a few cents worth of filament

75
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:50,720
 and a couple of hours of print time,

76
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:52,920
 we can hold in our hands a prototype of the part

77
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:54,880
 that we're about to get CNC machined

78
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:56,720
 or welded or otherwise manufactured

79
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,600
 that would probably cost a lot of money.

80
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:00,840
 And we can offer that up to the car or the boat

81
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:02,440
 or the rocket and check for fitment

82
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:05,200
 and check for other manufacturing and assembly issues.

83
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:06,760
 On a larger scale, there are some companies

84
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:09,080
 that maintain print farms to make finished components

85
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,800
 where they can't justify the initial upfront cost

86
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,080
 to get something injective moulded, for example.

87
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:16,040
 Prusa, a company who makes 3D printers

88
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:18,160
 incidentally the company who makes the 3D printers

89
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,840
 that we own down in TA for new teams.

90
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:22,360
 Prusa makes their 3D printers

91
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:25,400
 by printing components on other 3D printers.

92
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:26,760
 There's an excellent video with a tour

93
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:27,720
 through the Prusa factory

94
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:29,080
 where they take a look at their print farm

95
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:31,240
 at something like four or 500 printers

96
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,560
 that they use to print components

97
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:36,200
 for the thousands of printers that they ship out every week.

98
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:37,840
 And while this process is slower

99
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:39,640
 and doesn't provide the economic benefits

100
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:42,640
 at scale that other ones like say injection moulding might,

101
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:44,400
 this means that every time there's a design revision,

102
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,560
 Prusa doesn't need to again pay that upfront cost

103
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:47,920
 to have a new diamond in factured

104
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:50,080
 to have components injection moulded.

105
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,120
 In addition to convenience of this technique

106
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,080
 and accessibility of this technique for the every man

107
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,200
 and in addition to the low upfront costs

108
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:58,720
 in manufacturing with this technique

109
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:00,760
 and the kind of flexibility it gives you

110
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:04,040
 in rapid design and prototyping and updating of components.

111
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:05,880
 3D printing I think is particularly useful

112
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:07,600
 because it allows you to make some components

113
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:09,920
 that would otherwise be completely impossible

114
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:13,480
 or impractical and economical to make otherwise.

115
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:16,520
 This is true of both FDM 3D printed parts here in plastic

116
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,640
 but also of the metal 3D printed parts

117
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:20,440
 we talked about a few weeks ago.

118
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,240
 Some designs, particularly those made

119
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:25,000
 with new age design methods like topology optimization

120
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,840
 or generative design that look very organic,

121
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:30,160
 those parts are really hard to make using a technique

122
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:32,520
 like CNC machining or casting for example

123
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:35,560
 because they're so organic, they're so interesting,

124
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:36,880
 they're so weird.

125
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:38,960
 3D printing eats these designs up.

126
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,520
 All right, we've talked about the mechanics

127
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:41,840
 of how a 3D printer works

128
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,560
 and we've talked about some of the motivations

129
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:46,520
 for why you might 3D printer component in the first place.

130
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:48,280
 Let's talk about the actual process,

131
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:50,520
 how you go from a design that's in your head

132
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:52,600
 to a part that's going to be 3D printed.

133
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:54,920
 Like some forms of machining and laser cutting

134
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:58,080
 and plasma cutting, 3D printing is a CNC process,

135
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:00,240
 a computer numerically controlled process

136
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:02,000
 which means to 3D printer component,

137
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,280
 you need to start with some sort of 3D model

138
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,800
 of the thing you'd like to print.

139
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:07,240
 Oftentimes as a design engineer,

140
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,160
 you'll be making these models yourself, right?

141
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:10,640
 These will be the designs of the things

142
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,000
 that you want to put into service

143
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,240
 but occasionally especially if it's a standard component

144
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,120
 or an off-the-shelf and OEM component

145
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:18,240
 that you plan to incorporate into one of your designs,

146
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,920
 you may go find you can download a CAD file,

147
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:23,160
 a step file or an eye-just file of the component

148
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,000
 you'd like to print online.

149
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,320
 Irrespective of how you've come to have a CAD file

150
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:28,800
 of the thing that you'd like to print,

151
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,560
 the first tip is to convert this CAD file

152
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:32,440
 into a universal generic format

153
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,760
 that all 3D printer programs accept

154
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:36,920
 and STL, a stereo-lithy refile.

155
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:38,840
 STLs are approximations of your geometry,

156
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,560
 they're faceted solids, they're basically just a text file

157
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:45,000
 with a list of vertices and corresponding face information.

158
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,640
 And they allow you to package up complicated CAD geometry

159
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,360
 made up of extrudes and revolves and blends

160
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:53,120
 and other features into a reasonably simple approximation,

161
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,160
 what essentially amounts to a point cloud.

162
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:57,080
 To write the toolpaths, to tell the 3D printer

163
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:59,280
 how to move the head and when to extrude

164
00:05:59,280 --> 00:06:01,480
 molten thermoplastic onto the job,

165
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:03,440
 use a program called a slicer.

166
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:05,000
 A slicer which you run in your computer

167
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 takes an STL file as an input

168
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,160
 and allows you to control all sorts of settings

169
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:12,400
 about the print before finally converting this into G-code,

170
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:14,400
 the language that the printer is expecting

171
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:15,920
 that tells it how to move the head

172
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:19,080
 and how to move its axes to make up the path that you want.

173
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:20,280
 Most slicer softwares,

174
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:21,880
 even the reasonably straightforward ones

175
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:23,560
 that are included with some printers,

176
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,120
 will let you control a bunch of settings

177
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:27,080
 to do with the way that the thing is printed.

178
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:28,840
 For example, they'll let you control the layer height

179
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:31,320
 of the print, how much thermoplastic you dump out

180
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:33,800
 on each cross section and how far you move up

181
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,320
 between each cross section.

182
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:37,720
 As you can imagine, printing with corsa layer heights,

183
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:39,440
 thicker layer heights will result in a part

184
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:41,360
 with a maybe a porous surface finish

185
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,880
 and usually also some poorer mechanical properties,

186
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:45,400
 but we'll print much more quickly

187
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:46,880
 because there'll be far fewer layers

188
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:48,200
 for it to print in total.

189
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:49,840
 3D printers also let you control

190
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:52,920
 whether and how much infill is used in your prints.

191
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:54,440
 Especially when you're rapid prototyping,

192
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:57,040
 you may not want the inside of your print to be solid.

193
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:59,400
 You may not be putting any load on this prototype, for example,

194
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:01,160
 you may just be using to check fitment.

195
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:03,120
 In these cases, you may opt to print your part

196
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,280
 nearly hollow with only a few supports

197
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:08,840
 needed inside to kind of maintain the out of structure of it.

198
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:10,440
 In other cases, particularly if your part

199
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:13,200
 is a production part that is going to be put under some load,

200
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:15,840
 you may print closer to a solid geometry.

201
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:17,640
 Most slicer softwares will let you control

202
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:19,480
 not only the percentage of infill

203
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:20,720
 that you'd like in your part,

204
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:22,680
 what density you'd like your part to have, basically,

205
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:24,120
 but will also let you control

206
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,640
 what particular pattern it uses for the infill.

207
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:29,600
 Some of the most common being hexagonal patterns, for example.

208
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:31,800
 3D printers slicer softwares will also allow you

209
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,920
 to control the orientation of your part

210
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:37,360
 and whether or not any support material is printed as well.

211
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:38,800
 You can imagine, for some geometries,

212
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:41,160
 say a model of a person with their arms out,

213
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:42,800
 there'll be large areas of this print

214
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:45,520
 which are unsupported if you print from bottom to top.

215
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:48,200
 In this case, the arms of the person have no geometry,

216
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:49,640
 no material underneath them,

217
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,920
 and the 3D printer can't print on air.

218
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,000
 Consequently, 3D printing slicer softwares

219
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,800
 will detect unsupported areas of your print

220
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:58,760
 and offered to put in support material.

221
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:01,120
 Usually thin columns of plastic that will originate

222
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:02,680
 from the ground plane and come up

223
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:04,000
 to the thing you'd like to print.

224
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,320
 While these support materials are intentionally engineered

225
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,480
 to be easy to remove from the finished part,

226
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:10,800
 they can have somewhat of an impact on the strength

227
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,120
 of anything you're trying to print

228
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:13,320
 and the surface finish.

229
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:15,320
 So generally, it's considered good practice

230
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,400
 to try and minimize the amount of support material

231
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:19,320
 that's required when you're printing a part.

232
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:21,160
 Largely, you do this by altering the geometry

233
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:23,520
 to make sure you don't have large, overhanging areas,

234
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:25,320
 maybe by putting a gusset in, for example,

235
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:27,640
 but sometimes this isn't always possible.

236
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:30,280
 It can be addressed, however, by changing the orientation

237
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:31,880
 in which you print your part.

238
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:33,880
 A part printed in a particular orientation

239
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,840
 may require substantial support material

240
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:38,000
 to compensate for areas that are unsupported,

241
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,840
 but a simple rotation of this part

242
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:41,360
 to print in a different direction

243
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:43,760
 to use a different side of it as the ground plane

244
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:46,680
 might mean that no support material is needed at all.

245
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:48,880
 Now, selection of orientation to minimize

246
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:50,440
 the amount of support material required

247
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,560
 is quite often a trade-off in comparison

248
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,160
 to anisotropy introduced by the printing process.

249
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,200
 FDM 3D printed parts, because they constructed in layers,

250
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,200
 generally have a tendency to be stronger

251
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:04,440
 along a layer than they do to be across layers.

252
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:07,640
 This anisotropy occurs because in FDM 3D printing,

253
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:09,680
 a single layer is deposited in cools,

254
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,360
 roughly on the same time frame,

255
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:12,960
 whereas it's usually a substantial weight

256
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:15,320
 in between different layers that are deposited.

257
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,640
 Consequently, the plastic within a single layer

258
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,720
 is held together by reasonably strong bonds

259
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:22,560
 and the plastics between layers not so much.

260
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:24,360
 This means that when 3D printing a component

261
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,480
 which is going to be placed under some kind of load,

262
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:29,080
 you may wish to orient it when printing such that

263
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:32,320
 the load direction is in line with the layers

264
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:35,400
 of the print as opposed to perpendicular to the layers.

265
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:37,440
 Thus, the trade-off becomes apparent, right?

266
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,320
 There are some orientations which are particularly

267
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:41,000
 to printing because they minimise the amount

268
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:42,520
 of support material you need,

269
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:44,400
 and there are probably some other orientations

270
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:46,040
 which are quite good to printing because it means

271
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:48,960
 the layers of your 3D print end up oriented

272
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:50,880
 with the loads that you expect to see.

273
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:53,400
 Usually it's not the case that these two orientations

274
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:54,240
 are the same.

275
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:56,200
 When you need to deal with these competing objectives,

276
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:58,600
 you might look to turn the other knobs in the problem

277
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:01,280
 to try and achieve some sort of overall balance

278
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:03,280
 between strength and surface finish.

279
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:05,160
 You might, for example, print with your layers

280
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:06,880
 in the direction you expect the load to see,

281
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:08,640
 but to make up for the poor surface finish,

282
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:10,200
 you get from any support material,

283
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:13,040
 you might choose to print with finer layers to begin with.

284
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:15,560
 Or you might subject your 3D print as some sort of post-treatment

285
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:17,280
 to improve the surface finish.

286
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:20,320
 In general, design for 3D printing is an extremely broad field

287
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:22,680
 and we've only really scratched the surface here.

288
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:24,920
 You'll probably talk about it a little bit more in materials too,

289
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:27,200
 and hopefully in your later year design courses as well.

290
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:29,880
 But we also do a ton of 3D printing in a new team.

291
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:31,440
 So if you're interested in learning more,

292
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:33,760
 feel free to sign up and our 3D printing experts

293
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:35,360
 will be happy to talk to you about it.

294
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:37,000
 To close this video out, I might talk about some

295
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:38,960
 of the common FDM 3D printer filaments

296
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:40,360
 that you're likely to run into.

297
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:41,560
 We didn't talk about these last week

298
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:43,000
 when we met engineering plastics,

299
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,240
 but you should note that all of the FDM 3D printer

300
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:46,480
 filaments that you're likely to meet

301
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:47,880
 are going to be thermoplastics,

302
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:49,760
 because they need to be initially polymerized

303
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:51,920
 and then put onto a spool so that you can then

304
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:53,880
 melt them down at your extruder head.

305
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:55,720
 You can get FDM 3D printer filaments

306
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:57,120
 in a range of different flavors.

307
00:10:57,120 --> 00:10:59,560
 There are tens of hundreds of different types of filament

308
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:02,880
 you can buy, including the thermoplastics we met last week.

309
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:04,680
 That being said, here's a brief recap

310
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,240
 of the three most common.

311
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:09,880
 Ostensibly the most common is polylactic acid PLA.

312
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:12,160
 PLA is quite favorable for a bunch of different reasons,

313
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:13,960
 particularly it's low melting temperature,

314
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:16,080
 which means you don't need a particularly expensive

315
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:18,680
 or capable 3D printer to be able to melt it.

316
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:19,840
 And it's also quite nice

317
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:21,560
 because it doesn't give off any nasty fumes

318
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:23,680
 when it's printing, meaning that you can have a printer

319
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:26,200
 indoors around humans while it's going.

320
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:28,760
 PLA is quite a good starter material for 3D printing

321
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:31,560
 and even for rapid prototyping, I'd say it's a good choice.

322
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:33,960
 It's quite affordable, though it is a little bit brittle

323
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:36,040
 and tends to soften around 60 degrees C,

324
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:38,000
 so not particularly great for components

325
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,600
 that need to be added in temperature.

326
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,360
 ABS, a Krillo nitrile butadiene styrene,

327
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:44,480
 is another common 3D printer filament.

328
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:46,280
 It's been used in 3D printers for a long time,

329
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:48,360
 even before they were as mainstream as they are now.

330
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:50,360
 And you might recognize the name ABS

331
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:52,880
 because it's in a lot of other plastics as well.

332
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,720
 A really famous one is Lego bricks.

333
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:56,800
 ABS generally has slightly better mechanical

334
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:58,520
 and thermal performance than PLA.

335
00:11:58,520 --> 00:11:59,960
 It holds up at higher temperatures

336
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:01,160
 and it's a little bit stiffer

337
00:12:01,160 --> 00:12:02,800
 and a little bit stronger than PLA.

338
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:04,680
 But this is a bit of a two-edged sword.

339
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:06,160
 It has a higher service temperature

340
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:07,320
 and a higher melting temperature,

341
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:10,400
 but this also means you need a more substantial 3D printer

342
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:11,840
 to be able to melt it.

343
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:13,960
 And because there's a higher temperature between

344
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:15,480
 when it's molten and laid down

345
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,520
 and when it cools to room temperature,

346
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:20,040
 there's also quite a bit more thermal contraction

347
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:22,800
 and resulting distortion of your parts when you print them.

348
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,200
 The final common filament I'll touch on is Pet G,

349
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:27,080
 which is a glycol-modified version

350
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:28,960
 of the polyethylene terat bullet

351
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:30,480
 that we met last week when we talked

352
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:31,960
 about plastic water bottles.

353
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,680
 Pet G, like ABS, also has higher operating

354
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:35,680
 and melting temperatures

355
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:37,960
 and that requires a slightly hotter extruder

356
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:39,120
 and bed to deal with this.

357
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:41,400
 But it's a bit less prone to distortion than ABS

358
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:42,920
 as well as being a little bit stronger

359
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:44,200
 and a little bit stiffer.

360
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:46,720
 As I said, there are many, many different filaments available.

361
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:49,840
 And if you're going outside of PLA or ABS or Pet G,

362
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:51,440
 you've probably got a pretty good reason.

363
00:12:51,440 --> 00:12:53,440
 For FDM 3D printers, you can buy filaments

364
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:55,640
 that are really, really strong and really, really stiff.

365
00:12:55,640 --> 00:12:58,120
 You can buy filaments that have little bits of carbon fiber

366
00:12:58,120 --> 00:12:59,880
 or fiberglass chopped up into them.

367
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:01,880
 You can buy filaments which are very, very elastic,

368
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:03,080
 very, very flexible.

369
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,320
 And because of the huge variety of different filaments

370
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:07,640
 that are available, I won't touch on all of them here.

371
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:09,560
 Instead, I've left some really good resources

372
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:11,280
 for you on the textbook page for this video.

373
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:14,000
 And I hope that this is just the start of your journey

374
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,640
 when it comes to FDM 3D printing

375
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:18,240
 because it's very likely you're gonna use this technique

376
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:20,480
 very, very often as a design engineer.
